more than 200 000 people in the united  states have now died after contracting  the coronavirus  it is the highest number for any country  in the world even though the u.s  accounts for only about four percent of  the global population  in the capital washington the u.s house  of representatives held a moment of  silence for the victims  led by speaker nancy pelosi  rise for a moment of silence and  remembrance of those  more than 200 000 americans who have  passed away from the coba 19  virus pelosi has blamed the deaths on  the disinformation  and negligence in tackling the pandemic  by the white house  but president donald trump insists that  the u.s is rounding the corner in the  pandemic  a claim that he has been making for  months the high death toll is putting  his handling of the coronavirus outbreak  in the spotlight as he campaigns  for a second term in office  we asked u.s citizens in the city of  denver what they had to say  about this tragic milestone i mean i  don't understand how the richest country  in the world  could have such a an extensive public  health crisis  i feel like if we had real leadership  that  the number would be much smaller we  think trump has done a good job  i i think that there's a a story on the  other side that wants to say how bad  he's handled it but  nobody knew where nobody knew where it  was coming from in the beginning or what  it was all about  and dw correspondent stephan simon sets  us this update from washington on  whether the country is likely to change  its course in dealing with the pandemic  well the president didn't promise  anything now today or yesterday or last  week or the week before that  the president is busy campaigning saying  that he did  everything possible and more than  than that to curb the uh threat  and the uh voracious outcome of this  kovat19 pandemic or kovite 19  infections here in the united states so  on part of the president  everything is done very very well and he  saved  the lives of millions in his mind  democrats are  furious about how the president handled  this kovit 19 crisis here  and say 200 000 deaths  in in just doubling the number this  number just doubling in two or three  months  uh is a clear sign that the president  mishandled  uh this crisis this pandemic uh in in a  very very very very large and  significant way  i can't foresee any scenario that the  united states  at this moment will unify after those  numbers after today after this  milestone passing 200 000 deaths to  combat this  coronavirus pandemic in any other way  and that was stefan simons in washington  amid the criticism of his handling of  the pandemic president trump has lashed  out at china  blaming it for spreading the virus trump  went on the attack  in a pre-recorded message to the u.n  general assembly  as the world body marks 75 years since  its founding  the annual gathering took place with  coronavirus restrictions in place  the perfect riverside spot for a  fractious global family get together at  un headquarters  face masks and tight security even  though the most distinguished guests  joined by videolink a precaution to  avoid worsening the coronavirus pandemic  that has already killed almost one  million people worldwide  two a half empty hall of socially  distanced un  ambassadors un chief antonio guterres  warned about tensions between the u.s  and china  threatening to tear the organization and  the world  apart let us vanquish the five horsemen  our world cannot afford the future where  the two largest economies split the  globe in a great fracture  each with its own trade and financial  rules and internet and artificial  intelligence capacities  technological and economic divide risks  inevitably turning into a geostrategic  and military divide and we must avoid  these  at all costs and the increasingly  difficult relationship between the two  superpowers was on show as soon as  president trump's pre-recorded statement  started rolling  honor to address the united nations  general assembly  75. the u.s leader said the u.n should  hold beijing accountable for the covert  19  crisis and trump  championed his policy of putting his  country's national  interests  for first the same tired voices proposed  the same failed solutions  pursuing global ambitions at the expense  of their own people  as president i have rejected the failed  approaches of the past  and i am proudly putting america first  just as  you should be putting your countries  first  with early voting for the u.s  presidential elections already underway  in some u.s states  trump's speech attacking china on trade  and the environment  was aimed as much at u.s voters as other  world  leaders in contrast the pre-recorded  speeches from russia's president putin  and chinese president xi jinping urged a  global response to the coronavirus  pandemic  and more cooperation between countries  we should embrace the vision of a  community with a shared future  we should see each other as members of  the same big family  pursue win-win cooperation and not fall  into the trap of a clash of  civilizations  this year's general assembly debate  underscores how hard it is to keep  countries talking to  each other instead of past each other  we've heard so many times that the best  diplomacy is done  in person this year thanks to the  pandemic we're not getting that we're  getting diplomacy  i guess on zoom does that make a  difference  i i think it really does i think it  really is a real problem brent i mean  of course it's normal for these speeches  by the leaders  uh in the general debate to be used as  grandstanding opportunities we've  certainly seen plenty of that today  we're going to talk about that i'm sure  in a moment but still  like the event every year in new york  is unique in the diplomatic calendar  it's the only time when all of these  leaders get an opportunity to come  together and it's what happens  outside that chamber outside those  speeches that that really matters when  there are opportunities for the leaders  and also their teams to talk to each  other  and to have kind of serendipitous  encounters which you know can be  fruitful so it is a real concern  particularly for believers in diplomacy  believers in this the power of  multilateralism and we heard today  on beginning with president trump he  opened the assembly on the day that  america surpassed the 2000 mark  the death toll from the pandemic two  hundred thousand two hundred thousand  yeah and i mean that's the the highest  level in the world and of course  characteristically for donald trump  uh when he's open for criticism he goes  on the attack  and uh that's what he did very robustly  within virtually his first breath he was  talking about the china virus  now there are genuine concerns about  china in the early stages of the  outbreak in wuhan in the center of the  country at the end of last year  beginning of this about  how transparent they were with the world  health organization  also with with other countries about  that  um but still this was pretty aggressive  even by donald trump's standards  um and also it's only adding to these  fears that the us and china just headed  for a kind of cold war  scenario which the u.n secretary general  guterres  explicitly warned against in his speech  who was donald trump speaking to today i  want you to take  a look at what the group america first  policies posted immediately after donald  trump's speech  take a look as we speak the united  states is also working to end the war in  afghanistan  and we are bringing our troops home  america is fulfilling our destiny as  peacemaker  but it is peace through strength  so richard what do we have there  re-election campaign spot  i mean it it's not that much of a  surprise when we were watching it  live earlier it was a very content  kind of hit list of donald trump's view  of the world and what he sees as his  achievements you know donald trump's is  usually long  famously rambling this was wham bam bam  so it's not much of a surprise but i  mean bear in mind there is an election  in just a few weeks i think any  president who was  heading into an election like this would  use an event like this  to some extent uh to try and make some  political points but of course this was  in  trump's style let's talk a little bit  about global geopolitics and how  it's reflected at this meeting this is  what the russian president vladimir  putin said  today about the status quo if you will  take a look  russia has suggested convening a g5  summit  it would aim at reaffirming the key  principles of behaviour in international  affairs  elaborating ways to effectively address  today's most burning issues  it is encouraging that our partners have  supported the initiative  a g5 summit for the five members of the  security council what does that tell us  about the present day balance of powers  yeah well  well it's interesting because he's  standing up for a system that many say  is  is discredited that the u.n security  council is no longer functioning  that it is in a state of almost  permanent deadlock with  vladimir putin and xi jinping from china  on one side the united states on the  other side  and each of those having veto powers  which means that they can each block  each other's initiatives  and this has you know concrete  implications i mean look at the war in  syria that dragged on  uh the security council was unable to  get over those  veto hurdles to do anything meaningful  to end that conflict  and many smaller countries or a  particular grouping of smaller countries  of which germany is a member is saying  that we need to reform the u.n security  council that it needs to get bigger they  need to get rid of these vetoes  it needs to get more diverse but the  thing is and what we see from putin here  of course the vested interests and the  powers of those members of the u.n  security council are just so  powerful and no one likes to give up  their powers no exactly no one wants to  change  the status quo when it means they would  lose power and yet you've got  donald trump champion an american  retreat from multilateralism  we're seeing that at the same time  you've got the chinese president xi  jinping  showing the world that china is here to  preserve  the global order as we know it well  that's his  argument and of course it's an easier  argument to make when you have donald  trump there  essentially saying that um well he said  that  every country should stand up for itself  making the nationalist argument  of course china's critics would say well  you know  xi jinping at the same time is involved  in a border confrontation with india  it's cracking down on human rights in  hong kong it threatens  taiwan with invasion and it is trying to  expand its  its control within the south china sea  that these are not the acts  of a benign multilateralist power and  as we go to air richard i mean if you  look on the home page of the new york  times  or the washington post for that matter  you will really have to struggle to find  an article about  these speeches and the u.n general  assembly today what does it tell us does  the u.n does it still matter well i  noticed the same thing and it is  striking that  german news sites are featuring donald  trump's speech more prominently than  many  uh american ones um i think we do have  to  stress though that kind of american  neglect and disinterest in the united  nations is nothing that new  i mean you remember back in 1997 ted  turner the founder of cnn  donated a billion dollars to the united  nations  at a time when congress had stopped  paying its dues  george bush nominated as the ambassador  to the un  uh a man who had said the best thing you  could do to the un was  to knock off a bunch of stories from the  building so  it's not so new i think um what's more  important really is  is or the bigger challenge to the un  at the moment is that so many countries  with the us at the pinnacle are led by  uh leaders who question the very idea  of international cooperation yeah that's  right there's a lot lots of enemies  right now of  multilateralism and those enemies are in  power around the world  richard walker our chief international  editor richard thank you  you

